#import "../lib.typ": *

#section(
  exercise([
    #drill-heading(61)

    This copy furnishes all the movement drill necessary in beginning this
    lesson.

    Count ten for each drill, two for capital S, and eight for the reverse
    traced oval. This drill is especially recommended to those who find the
    development of a light, quick movement difficult.

    #instruction[Write sixteen exercises in 60 seconds.]
  ], lines: 6, slots: 5, examples: ("drill-61", "drill-61-2")),
  exercise([
    #drill-heading(62)

    Study the curve of the first stroke; study the loop at the top, and give
    special attention to the fact that the loop (upper part of S) is one-half
    the entire length of the letter. Use an easy, light movement. Count 1, 2,
    for each letter.

    #instruction[Write forty-five to fifty S's in 60 seconds.]
  ], lines: 6, slots: 2, examples: ("drill-62", "drill-62-2")),
  exercise([
    #drill-heading(63)

    The angular finishing stroke shown in drill sixty-three is very popular
    with many excellent teachers of business writing. Its practical feature is
    the direction taken by the finishing stroke, which may be joined to any
    letter following. Count 1, 2, swing; or 1, 2, 3, for each letter.

    #instruction[Write forty-five to fifty S's in 60 seconds.]
  ], lines: 6, examples: ("drill-63", "drill-63-2")),
  exercise([
    #drill-heading(64)

    Write a page of this copy; more if you have sufficient time. A continuous
    steady movement should be used. Do not lift the pen from the beginning to
    the ending of the word.
  ], lines: 6, slots: 2, examples: ("drill-64", "drill-64-2")),
)
